Semantics I deliberately preserved:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mfread(in, 1, 1000, stdin)[0m — reads raw bytes across newlines and loops until the buffer fills or EOF, so the Rust side loops on [38;5;10mRead::read[0m rather than reading a line or slurping everything. Read errors are ignored, as in the C.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mchar in[1000] = ""[0m is fully zero-filled, so short input is NUL-terminated by the zero fill. An embedded NUL in the input truncates the counted string — my version stops at the first NUL too ([38;5;10mt_nul_early[0m, [38;5;10mt_nul_first[0m confirm).[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mstrchr[0m-based loop counts every occurrence; [38;5;10ms++[0m after each hit means overlapping is not an issue, and the count is a plain [38;5;10mint[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One case is genuinely undefined in the original: exactly 1000 bytes of input fills the buffer with no terminator, and [38;5;10mstrchr[0m walks off the end. I cap the scan at 1000 bytes. Both binaries agreed on my test, but that agreement is luck of the stack contents, not a guarantee — there is no defined behavior to match there.[0m[0m
